{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mlktxy6gs5hk4xk43xahaigibdcvs5gdyvb62sgwi2kcdt6wukkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. The Rust translation matches the C byte-for-byte on every input tested.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols | ✅ C exports exactly `call_predict`; Rust `.so` exports it. `comm -23` empty for all 4 cdylib variants. No stubs. |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row passes across randomized inputs | ✅ 28 rows, fixed-seed SplitMix64 |\n| Binary stdout comparison | N/A — no `add_executable`, no `[[bin]]` |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing differential test | ✅ 17 rows |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ 6/6 (`{default, --no-default-features, internal_probe}` × `{debug, release}`) |\n\n## What I found and fixed\n\n`cargo check` was clean and the pre-existing translation of `call_predict` was already correct — no logic divergences were found. The real gaps were in **verification coverage**, and I closed them:\n\n1. **The 14 `static` C helpers had zero test coverage.** `call_predict` only *compares function addresses* — it never invokes a predictor — so all the predictor arithmetic (16 switch arms + 12 specialised variants) was invisible to any test that can only call the exported symbol. I added `tests/cprobe/probe.c`, which `#include`s the **unmodified** `c_src/src/lib.c` to give the statics external linkage, plus a test-only `internal_probe` Cargo feature exposing matching `probe_*` Rust exports. Both `.so`s are loaded with `libloading` and compared function-by-function. All 7 tests pass, including:\n   - `btac1c_idxstate` layout parity (size/align/`offset_of(firfx)`) — a mismatch would silently make the FIR arm read wrong coefficients\n   - the C source's deliberate asymmetry (`_Pfn10` uses `>>3` where switch arm 10 uses `>>4`; `_Pfn11` uses `>>1` vs `>>3`) — the test *asserts the difference is observed*, so a translation that \"fixed\" the C would fail\n   - negative/small-magnitude samples, which distinguish arithmetic `>>` from truncating `/`\n2. **Harness bug I introduced and then fixed:** `cargo test` does *not* build a `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` lib for integration tests, so my first harness silently loaded a **stale** `target/debug/*.so` (built without the feature). Each variant now builds freshly into its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` — this also means row 20 genuinely tests both profiles rather than testing release twice.\n\nTwo notes worth flagging: `lib.h` declares `get_predict_func` but the C never defines it, so I added a test asserting **neither** library exports it (a stub would be a false positive). And crates.io is unreachable from the sandbox — `libloading 0.8.9` was already in the cargo cache, so all commands use `--offline`; `run_all_combos.sh` bakes that in."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":661,"cache_read_input_tokens":105102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":661,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"98ae4e8c-bc9e-46da-bfdb-152524d2f32b","uuid":"afbc0671-a620-4c2d-9f22-812f8fdff5df","timestamp":"2026-09-05T06:47:32.126Z"}
